{"id": 3751697, "title": "CVE-2026-10536: HTTP/2 stream-dependency tree UAF", "submitted": "2026-05-20", "source_url": "https://hackerone.com/reports/3751697", "excerpt": "# Use-after-free in `curl_easy_reset()` with HTTP/2 stream-dependency tree\n\nHi all,\n\nWe've found an issue in `lib/easy.c` where `curl_easy_reset()` bypasses `data_priority_cleanup()` before clearing `data->set`, leaving the HTTP/2 stream-dependency tree with dangling pointers to the reset handle.\n\nThe current `curl_easy_reset()` at `lib/easy.c:1090-1098` does:\n\n```c\nCurl_freeset(data);\nmemset(&data->set, 0, sizeof(struct UserDefined));\nCurl_init_userdefined(data);\n```\n\nThis zeroes `data->set.priority.{parent,children}` without first walking the dependency tree to remove this handle from its peers' children lists. After a subsequent `curl_easy_cleanup()`, peers still reference the freed handle through their `priority.children` list and dereference it from `data_priority_cleanup` → `priority_remove_child` (`lib/url.c:3094, 3022`).\n\nThe defect dates to commit `71b7e01610` (2022-12-30, \"lib: connect/h2/h3 refactor\"), which converted `set.priority` into a linked tree and introduced `Curl_data_priority_cleanup()` in `lib/url.c`, but did not add a call to it from `curl_easy_reset()`. Reachable through documented public APIs only, on any `USE_NGHTTP2` build.\n\n## Reproducer\n\nBuild curl with ASan:\n\n```sh\nautoreconf -fi\nmkdir build && cd build\nCFLAGS='-O1 -g -fsanitize=address' LDFLAGS='-fsanitize=address' CC=clang \\\n  ../configure --with-openssl --enable-debug --enable-maintainer-mode --disable-shared\nmake -j\n```\n\nSave as `poc.c`:\n\n```c\n#include <curl/curl.h>\nint main(void) {\n    curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_DEFAULT);\n    CURL *A = curl_easy_init();\n    CURL *B = curl_easy_init();\n    curl_easy_setopt(B, CURLOPT_STREAM_DEPENDS, A);\n    curl_easy_reset(B);\n    curl_easy_cleanup(B);\n    curl_easy_cleanup(A);\n    curl_global_cleanup();\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nCompile and run:\n\n```sh\nclang -fsanitize=address -g -I../include poc.c \\\n  ./lib/.libs/libcurl.a -lssl -lcrypto -lz -lnghttp2 -lidn2 -lzstd \\\n  -lbrotlidec -lldap -llber -o poc\n./poc\n```\n\n## Observed output\n\n```\n=================================================================\n==654355==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free on address 0x7ac36ebe2148 at pc 0x614bec3b354f bp 0x7fff212fbea0 sp 0x7fff212fbe98\nREAD of size 8 at 0x7ac36ebe2148 thread T0\n    #0 priority_remove_child   lib/url.c:3022:3\n    #1 data_priority_cleanup   lib/url.c:3094:5\n    #2 Curl_close              lib/url.c:278:3\n    #3 curl_easy_cleanup", "excerpt_truncated": true, "final": "likely_valid", "final_label": "Confirmed by the code", "tone": "good", "explain": "The real code shows the flaw the report describes.", "confidence": 0.92, "judge_verdict": "likely_valid", "receipts_override": "", "policy_section": "", "grounding": {"verdict": "grounded", "rev": "d3b04e560036", "rev_source": "master on 2026-05-20", "counts": {"grounded": 21, "ungrounded_strong": 0, "ungrounded_medium": 0}, "problems": []}, "summary": "Use-after-free in curl_easy_reset() when handling HTTP/2 stream-dependency tree, leading to dangling pointer dereference in data_priority_cleanup after reset.", "claims": [{"id": "C1", "kind": "flaw", "text": "curl_easy_reset() bypasses data_priority_cleanup() before clearing data->set, leaving dangling pointers in the HTTP/2 stream-dependency tree", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "curl_easy_reset() clears data->set (including priority.parent and priority.children) via memset at line 1097 without first calling data_priority_cleanup() to unlink the handle from its peers' children lists. The function data_priority_cleanup exists at lib/url.c:3090 but is only called from Curl_close (lib/url.c:278), not from curl_easy_reset.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/easy.c", "line": 1090, "note": "curl_easy_reset calls Curl_req_hard_reset, Curl_hash_clean, Curl_meta_reset", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/easy.c", "line": 1096, "note": "Curl_freeset(data) called", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/easy.c", "line": 1097, "note": "memset(&data->set, 0, sizeof(struct UserDefined)) zeros priority pointers", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/easy.c", "line": 1098, "note": "Curl_init_userdefined(data) re-initializes", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 3090, "note": "data_priority_cleanup exists but is not called from curl_easy_reset", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C2", "kind": "location", "text": "defect introduced in commit 71b7e01610 (2022-12-30) when converting set.priority into a linked tree and adding Curl_data_priority_cleanup() but not calling it from curl_easy_reset()", "status": "not verifiable", "tone": "muted", "reasoning": "The deterministic checks confirm the current code state but do not include git commit history. The claim about commit 71b7e01610 cannot be verified from the provided sources.", "citations": []}, {"id": "C3", "kind": "trigger", "text": "reachable through documented public APIs only, on any USE_NGHTTP2 build", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The priority tree code is compiled only when USE_NGHTTP2 is defined (lib/url.c:3014, 3089). The reproducer uses only documented public APIs: curl_easy_init, curl_easy_setopt with CURLOPT_STREAM_DEPENDS, curl_easy_reset, and curl_easy_cleanup.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 3014, "note": "#ifdef USE_NGHTTP2 guards the priority tree implementation", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 3089, "note": "#ifdef USE_NGHTTP2 guards data_priority_cleanup definition", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/easy.c", "line": 1084, "note": "curl_easy_reset is a public API", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C4", "kind": "impact", "text": "dangling pointer dereference in data_priority_cleanup() leading to heap-use-after-free when peers reference freed handle through priority.children list", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "When curl_easy_reset(B) zeros B's priority pointers but leaves B in A's children list, subsequent curl_easy_cleanup(B) frees B's memory. Then curl_easy_cleanup(A) calls Curl_close → data_priority_cleanup → priority_remove_child, which reads and writes through the dangling pointer to the freed B struct (lib/url.c:3022, 3034-3035). The ASan output confirms heap-use-after-free at these lines.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 3092, "note": "data_priority_cleanup walks data->set.priority.children", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 3094, "note": "calls priority_remove_child(data, tmp) where tmp is child->data", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 3022, "note": "priority_remove_child reads child->set.priority.parent (DEBUGASSERT)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 3034, "note": "priority_remove_child writes child->set.priority.parent = 0", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 3035, "note": "priority_remove_child writes child->set.priority.exclusive = FALSE", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C5", "kind": "reproduction", "text": "reproducer uses curl_easy_reset() on a stream-dependent handle followed by curl_easy_cleanup() causing ASan to report heap-use-after-free in priority_remove_child", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The reproducer's sequence (init A, init B, set B depends on A, reset B, cleanup B, cleanup A) exercises the exact path: reset zeros priority without unlinking, first cleanup frees the handle, second cleanup walks the stale children list and triggers the use-after-free. The ASan output in the report matches the code locations.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/easy.c", "line": 1084, "note": "curl_easy_reset public API", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/easy.c", "line": 844, "note": "curl_easy_cleanup calls Curl_close", "valid": true}]}], "external_checks": [], "external_sources": [{"url": "https://issues.guix.gnu.org/29738", "title": "Add support for HTTP/2 to curl", "for": "The defect dates to commit 71b7e01610 (2022-12-30, \"lib: connect/h2/h3 refactor\")"}, {"url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/purview/priority-cleanup-exchange", "title": "Use priority cleanup to expedite the permanent deletion of sensitive information from mailboxes | Microsoft Learn", "for": "Expose the existing static data_priority_cleanup helper and call it from curl_easy_reset, the same way Curl_close already does"}, {"url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/purview/priority-cleanup-onedrive-sharepoint", "title": "Override holds to clean up files for Copilot and reclaim storage | Microsoft Learn", "for": "Expose the existing static data_priority_cleanup helper and call it from curl_easy_reset, the same way Curl_close already does"}], "key_reasons": ["curl_easy_reset() clears data->set.priority via memset (lib/easy.c:1097) without calling data_priority_cleanup() to unlink the handle from its parent's children list", "Curl_close() correctly calls data_priority_cleanup() (lib/url.c:278) but curl_easy_reset() does not", "When a reset handle is later cleaned up, its memory is freed; a subsequent cleanup of its former parent walks the stale children list and dereferences the freed memory in priority_remove_child (lib/url.c:3022, 3034-3035)", "The reproducer uses only documented public APIs and triggers the flaw on any USE_NGHTTP2 build", "Use-after-free is a memory safety violation (CWE-416) reachable through valid API usage, not API misuse"], "missing_info": [], "reply": "Thank you for the detailed report and reproducer. The code confirms that curl_easy_reset() zeros the priority tree pointers (lib/easy.c:1097) without first calling data_priority_cleanup() to unlink the handle from its peers, while Curl_close() does call it (lib/url.c:278). This leaves dangling pointers that cause a heap-use-after-free when the parent is later cleaned up (lib/url.c:3022, 3034-3035). The fix to expose Curl_data_priority_cleanup and call it from curl_easy_reset is correct and matches the existing pattern in Curl_close. We will address this.", "code": [{"name": "lib/easy.c", "start": 1065, "end": 1123, "reason": "lines 1090-1098 cited by the report", "rows": [{"n": 1065, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 1066, "text": "  if(outcurl) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 1067, "text": "#ifndef CURL_DISABLE_COOKIES", "notes": []}, {"n": 1068, "text": "    curlx_free(outcurl->cookies);", "notes": []}, {"n": 1069, "text": "#endif", "notes": []}, {"n": 1070, "text": "    curlx_dyn_free(&outcurl->state.headerb);", "notes": []}, {"n": 1071, "text": "    Curl_altsvc_cleanup(&outcurl->asi);", "notes": []}, {"n": 1072, "text": "    Curl_hsts_cleanup(&outcurl->hsts);", "notes": []}, {"n": 1073, "text": "    Curl_freeset(outcurl);", "notes": []}, {"n": 1074, "text": "    curlx_free(outcurl);", "notes": []}, {"n": 1075, "text": "  }", "notes": []}, {"n": 1076, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 1077, "text": "  return NULL;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1078, "text": "}", "notes": []}, {"n": 1079, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 1080, "text": "/*", "notes": []}, {"n": 1081, "text": " * curl_easy_reset() is an external interface that allows an app to re-", "notes": []}, {"n": 1082, "text": " * initialize a session handle to the default values.", "notes": []}, {"n": 1083, "text": " */", "notes": []}, {"n": 1084, "text": "void curl_easy_reset(CURL *curl)", "notes": ["C3: curl_easy_reset is a public API", "C5: curl_easy_reset public API"]}, {"n": 1085, "text": "{", "notes": []}, {"n": 1086, "text": "  struct Curl_easy *data = curl;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1087, "text": "  if(!GOOD_EASY_HANDLE(data))", "notes": []}, {"n": 1088, "text": "    return;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1089, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 1090, "text": "  Curl_req_hard_reset(&data->req, data);", "notes": ["C1: curl_easy_reset calls Curl_req_hard_reset, Curl_hash_clean, Curl_meta_reset"]}, {"n": 1091, "text": "  Curl_hash_clean(&data->meta_hash);", "notes": []}, {"n": 1092, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 1093, "text": "  /* clear all meta data